1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:17,000
 In this chapter we will study depreciation methods. First we will talk a little bit about the terminology, next we will see four important depreciation methods and we will briefly touch upon depletion methods.

2
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:32,000
 So what is depreciation about? Well depreciation is about a reduction in value over time of an asset. That reduction in value can be due to wear and tear, to use, to deterioration, to obsolescence.

3
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:40,000
 And the method to work with that depreciation is depreciation.

4
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:53,000
 Important to note is that depreciation does not represent an actual cash flow, nor does it necessarily reflect the actual use pattern of the asset during the ownership.

5
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:10,000
 There is something like tax depreciation, and that is what you are allowed to do by the government, and it determines partly the amount of taxes you have to pay. So it does not reflect necessarily how you have been using the asset.

6
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:25,000
 Terminology, there is nothing very special about it. The first cost or unadjusted base, that is what we have been calling P so far. We have the book value, that is new.

7
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:38,000
 That is the unadjusted base, the first cost, with the adjustments made for the depreciations, the recovery period, the market value, salvage value.

8
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:55,000
 Depreciation, there is a depreciation rate, which is determining the amount of depreciation. You multiply it by the book value or by the unadjusted basis, and then you have your depreciation amount.

9
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:00,000
 Personal property and real property, slightly different approaches.

10
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:14,000
 The depreciation methods that we will study here are the single line, which is the most simple one, the declining balance and the double declining balance, which is an accelerated method.

11
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:25,000
 The other two are also accelerated methods. Some of your digits are here, it is not used so much anymore, but in order to be complete we see it here.

12
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:38,000
 And then the modified accelerated cost recovery system, which is a US model. It is described in the book, other countries have similar systems.

13
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:54,000
 Straight line depreciation, as the name is saying, it is a system that is taking into account a book value decrease that is linear with time.

14
00:02:54,000 --> 00:03:00,000
 Depreciation rate is 1 over n, and it is staying that way.

15
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:18,000
 So there you see the amount of depreciation in year T, it is b minus s, so our unadjusted basis, our p, minus a salvage value, and then multiplied by d, and d is 1 over n.

16
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:27,000
 And the book value in a given year is then b minus d times dt.

17
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:38,000
 Maybe a little example, so we have a situation in which we have a first cost of 50,000, so that is our b value.

18
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:49,000
 We have a 10,000 estimated salvage value, which is our s after 5 years, and we are asked to calculate the annual depreciation and to compute and plot the book value.

19
00:03:50,000 --> 00:04:01,000
 Simple, well we know what dt is, it is b minus s over n, and it is constant.

20
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:17,000
 So that means that after 1 year our book value is 50,000, our starting b minus 1 time our depreciation, and after 5 years we have 50,000, our starting b minus 5 times our depreciation,

21
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:23,000
 and that brings us to 10,000, our salvage value, and you see we have a straight line.

22
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:36,000
 The declining balance and the double declining balance depreciation are accelerated methods, which means that they are going faster than linear, at least in the beginning that is.

23
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:41,000
 They are also known as fixed percentage or uniform percentage methods.

24
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:57,000
 The annual depreciation here is determined by multiplying the book value at the beginning of the year by a fixed percentage, and thus the depreciation amount decreases year by year, because of course the book value is decreasing year by year.

25
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:05,000
 It does not use an estimated salvage value, it is its own implied salvage value.

26
